window.__labLoaded({"id":"9.3","title":"Bayesian and Frequentist Thinking: Two Ways to Learn from Data","goal":"Update a belief about a checkout page's conversion rate (14 buyers out of 40) with a Beta prior, get a 95% credible interval, and compare it with the usual confidence interval.","python":{"blocks":[{"title":"Prior, data and posterior","text":"A Beta(a, b) prior acts like a past record of a successes and b failures. Beta(1, 1) is flat: every rate is equally plausible. After k buyers out of n, the posterior is simply Beta(a + k, b + n - k).","code":"import numpy as np\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nfrom scipy import stats\n\na, b = 1, 1\nk, n = 14, 40\n\npost_a, post_b = a + k, b + n - k\nposterior = stats.beta(post_a, post_b)\n\nprint(f'posterior = Beta({post_a}, {post_b})')\nprint(f'posterior mean = {posterior.mean():.3f}')","out":"posterior = Beta(15, 27)\nposterior mean = 0.357","figs":[]},{"title":"Credible interval and a direct probability","text":"A 95% credible interval takes the middle 95% of the posterior. We can also read off the probability that the new page beats the old 25% rate, which a p-value cannot give directly.","code":"low, high = posterior.ppf([0.025, 0.975])\np_better = posterior.sf(0.25)\n\nprint(f'95% credible interval: {low:.3f} to {high:.3f}')\nprint(f'P(rate > 25%) = {p_better:.3f}')","out":"95% credible interval: 0.221 to 0.506\nP(rate > 25%) = 0.933","figs":[]},{"title":"The frequentist answer for comparison","text":"The usual approach tests the null value 25% and builds a confidence interval (Wilson). The numbers look alike, but the sentences mean different things: the confidence interval is about repeating the procedure, the credible interval about this rate.","code":"from statsmodels.stats.proportion import proportion_confint\n\nci = proportion_confint(k, n, alpha=0.05, method='wilson')\nse = np.sqrt(0.25 * 0.75 / n)\nz = (k / n - 0.25) / se\n\nprint(f'Wilson 95% CI: {ci[0]:.3f} to {ci[1]:.3f}')\nprint(f'z = {z:.2f}, one-sided p = {stats.norm.sf(z):.3f}')","out":"Wilson 95% CI: 0.221 to 0.505\nz = 1.46, one-sided p = 0.072","figs":[]},{"title":"Draw the prior and the posterior","text":"The flat prior is a level line. The data pull the belief into a peak near 0.35, and the shaded band is the 95% credible interval. The dashed line marks the old page's 25% rate.","code":"x = np.linspace(0, 1, 400)\n\nplt.figure(figsize=(6, 3.4))\nplt.plot(x, stats.beta(a, b).pdf(x), color='#7f7f7f', label='prior Beta(1, 1)')\nplt.plot(x, posterior.pdf(x), color='#1f77b4', label=f'posterior Beta({post_a}, {post_b})')\nmid = (x >= low) & (x <= high)\nplt.fill_between(x[mid], posterior.pdf(x[mid]), color='#1f77b4', alpha=0.2)\nplt.axvline(0.25, color='#d62728', linestyle='--', label='old rate 25%')\nplt.xlabel('Conversion rate')\nplt.ylabel('Density')\nplt.legend()\nplt.show()","out":"","figs":["9.3-4.png"]}]},"r":{"code":"qbeta(c(0.025, 0.975), 15, 27)\n1 - pbeta(0.25, 15, 27)\n15 / (15 + 27)\nprop.test(14, 40, correct = FALSE)$conf.int\nprop.test(14, 40, p = 0.25, alternative = \"greater\", correct = FALSE)$p.value","expect":"credible interval 0.2212 0.5059; P(rate > 0.25) = 0.9334; posterior mean 0.3571; Wilson 95% CI 0.2213 0.5049; one-sided p-value = 0.0721","note":"prop.test with correct = FALSE gives the Wilson score interval and the plain z test, matching the Python. The Bayesian numbers come straight from the Beta distribution with qbeta and pbeta."},"spss":{"menu":"SPSS has no standard menu for Bayesian proportions, so use Transform > Compute Variable with the IDF.BETA and CDF.BETA functions","syntax":"DATA LIST FREE / dummy.\nBEGIN DATA\n1\nEND DATA.\nCOMPUTE lower = IDF.BETA(0.025, 15, 27).\nCOMPUTE upper = IDF.BETA(0.975, 15, 27).\nCOMPUTE p_better = 1 - CDF.BETA(0.25, 15, 27).\nCOMPUTE z = (0.35 - 0.25) / SQRT(0.25 * 0.75 / 40).\nCOMPUTE p_onesided = 1 - CDF.NORMAL(z, 0, 1).\nFORMATS lower upper p_better z p_onesided (F8.4).\nLIST lower upper p_better z p_onesided.","note":"Bayesian analysis is not a standard menu in SPSS, so the Beta(15, 27) posterior is calculated directly (the 15 and 27 come from the flat prior plus 14 buyers and 26 non-buyers). The Wilson confidence interval is not a standard SPSS output, so only the z test is shown."},"stata":{"code":"display \"posterior mean = \" 15 / 42\ndisplay \"lower 95% = \" invibeta(15, 27, 0.025)\ndisplay \"upper 95% = \" invibeta(15, 27, 0.975)\ndisplay \"P(rate > 0.25) = \" 1 - ibeta(15, 27, 0.25)\ncii proportions 40 14, wilson\nprtesti 40 14 0.25","note":"invibeta(a, b, p) is the inverse Beta CDF and ibeta(a, b, x) the Beta CDF. cii proportions with the wilson option gives the Wilson interval, and prtesti runs the z test (read the Ha: p > 0.25 line, p about 0.072). These commands need no data in memory."},"try":["Use the sceptical prior Beta(5, 15): set a, b = 5, 15 and rerun. Predict whether P(rate > 25%) goes up or down.","Use 140 buyers out of 400 (the same 35%). Predict what happens to the width of the credible interval and to the effect of the prior."]});
